 <section class="economics">
 <h2>Does the money work?</h2>
-<p>The first number is now a screen. A current employer advertises €2,500–€4,375 gross per month for a 32–40 hour DIA role. <!-- CHECK: 06-dia-salary --> Use a <strong>€75,000 loaded annual retention-cost hurdle</strong> for a senior signing DIA after salary, employer costs, pension, vehicle and equipment. Only the salary range is observed. The loaded amount is deliberately conservative required-price arithmetic.</p>
-<p>If the seller is the only technical final responsible person and their labour is missing from payroll, reported EBITDA must be at least <strong>€150,000 before that normalisation</strong>. Subtract the €75,000 role cost; otherwise the signature consumes more than half the profit. If a senior DIA's full cost is already in payroll, count only the incremental retention package. This settles the pitch's number as a due-diligence rule, not as a claim about any unnamed target.</p>
+<p>The first number is now a screen. A current employer advertises €2,500–€4,375 per month for a 32–40 hour DIA role. <!-- CHECK: 06-dia-salary --> Use a <strong>€75,000 loaded annual retention-cost hurdle</strong> for a senior signing DIA after salary, employer costs, pension, vehicle and equipment. Only the advertised salary range is observed. The loaded amount is deliberately conservative required-price arithmetic.</p>
+<p>Reported EBITDA is not the valuation base when the seller works inside it. Build the bridge below from target payroll, time records and replacement quotes. Allocate the seller's hours across technical, management and customer work, without charging one hour twice. Subtract founder labour only when it is a distinct operating role. This settles the pitch's retention-cost number target by target.</p>
+<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
+<thead><tr><th>Seller-to-buyer bridge</th><th>Evidence or rule</th></tr></thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Reported EBITDA</td><td>Target accounts</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Plus actual seller pay and valid one-off add-backs</td><td>Payroll, tax records and invoices</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Less missing DIA and technical-signatory work</td><td>Target-derived replacement quote; compare with the €75k hurdle</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Less missing management, sales and account work</td><td>Target-derived hours and replacement quote</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Less buyer/founder labour, only if distinct</td><td>€85k replacement-cost hurdle in the operating design</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Like-for-like normalised EBITDA</strong></td><td><strong>Only this measure enters the multiple</strong></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Less debt service, tax, capex and eligible earn-out</td><td>Company cash available to the owner</td></tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
 <p>Every figure in the table is a <strong>design hurdle</strong>, not an observed target result or market forecast. The only observed customer-price comparators are the Asdesk figures above. “Job” can range from a simple building to a larger scoped assignment, so invoices and technician time must replace the blended assumptions before a bid.</p>
 <div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
 <thead><tr><th>€000</th><th>First year after closing</th><th>Mature operating design</th></tr></thead>
