 <p>The first year is thin even after paying the founder €85,000 at replacement cost. Five hundred jobs mean about five completed jobs per DIA per week across fifty working weeks. The model dies if the verified job mix cannot average €900 before VAT, if reporting time lowers capacity, or if rework and samples exceed the allowances.</p>
-<p>The acquisition-price hurdle is <strong>€300,000</strong>: no more than €50,000 reader equity at risk, a €200,000 loan and a €50,000 seller earn-out paid only when retained staff and customer gross profit persist. Qredits currently publishes €50,000–€250,000 MKB credit, 9.95% annual interest, 1.5% one-off cost, an average five-year term and acquisition as an allowed use. <!-- CHECK: 06-qredits-terms --> A €200,000 five-year annuity at that published rate is about €50,900 annual debt service; that calculation is not an approval or quote.</p>
-<p>Brookz's broad construction-and-installation guidance gives an indicative 4.3–5.5 EBITDA multiple. Its visible asbest page showed two much larger active businesses and one sold inventory bureau at €850,000 revenue and 6–10 staff. <!-- CHECK: 06-sale-pool --> <!-- CHECK: 06-multiple --> The record did not show an affordable two-DIA firm, a completed price or verified profit. At the low multiple, €70,000 normalised operating surplus supports roughly the €300,000 hurdle. If the seller applies the multiple before paying replacement labour, walk away.</p>
-<p>Working capital needs its own reserve. Bill scheduled portfolio work monthly, collect deposits on private jobs and keep customer-funded laboratory work short-cycle. The €50,000 equity cannot all become goodwill. Legal, financial, tax, employment and certificate diligence, the lender fee and at least one month of payroll must fit inside the purchase structure. No deal closes if doing so pushes reader capital at risk above €50,000.</p>
+<p>The <strong>€275,000 purchase-price hurdle</strong> is lower than the total funding package. Qredits currently publishes €50,000–€250,000 MKB credit, 9.95% annual interest, 1.5% one-off cost, an average five-year term and acquisition as an allowed use. <!-- CHECK: 06-qredits-terms --> A €200,000 five-year annuity at that rate is about €50,900 yearly. The 1.5% fee is €3,000. Both calculations are arithmetic, not approval or quotes.</p>
